#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128935 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fad1a9a-7860-5c31-8b3a-e9279f88965b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Cylindrical Polar Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "45c727f9-ab15-5c6f-a34b-f9b1171e110d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6219" :question/problem "What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $N$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1981/Source/Images/deac7441155c1440a00a786c590e7f03.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z)$, where\n\n$$\nr \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1981/Source/Images/5ea6b167ebe524618c905f0a1fe8f041.png)\n\n- The ray from the origin through $N'$ makes an angle of $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The distance from the origin to $N'$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}$.\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=3$ because $N$ lies $3$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $N$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, 3\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cartesian Coordinates to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "7ba9f05c-9c5d-518c-bf76-ba1164c49d2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6220" :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2)$. What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A$?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z)$, where\n\n$$\nr \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are given as\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2)\n$$\n\n- From these Cartesian coordinates, we know that $z=\\sqrt{2}$.\n- To find the values of $r$ and $\\theta$, we need to find the polar coordinates of the point $(x,y) = (\\sqrt 3, -3)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2} + (-3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{12} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(\\sqrt3,-3)$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we first need to find the reference angle $\\theta_R$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{y}{x} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan (\\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}, \\sqrt2 \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128937 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb1182f-76ed-514d-808a-94cad16bc2c7" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cylindrical Polar Coordinates to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "3e024f68-63e5-5080-ba74-c806f6af9036" :question/math-academy-id "e-6221" :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $\\left(4, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, -8 \\right)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 4\\cos (\\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= 4\\sin (\\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt3, 2, -8)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cartesian Coordinates to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb1f6776-1674-5bd7-bc41-5cd3a90f840b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128571} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4ad79-6997-5b94-800a-d3c90041f633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128572} {:answer/id #uuid "364a29ba-4e0e-5105-8321-cd1033689662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{3π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128573} {:answer/id #uuid "5f01eb79-9c4a-5d9a-9ea6-bd4112fb0370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{7π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128574} {:answer/id #uuid "63e7b00e-3fcd-5334-9f56-405f03293ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{7π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e7b00e-3fcd-5334-9f56-405f03293ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{7π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128575} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fedb99c-36c2-5604-b5ed-89fa5e1f98d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128570}] :question/id #uuid "41fddd1f-73c8-5199-8626-6f5cca7bf9be" :question/math-academy-id "q-74297" :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(2,-2, 5)$. What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb1182f-76ed-514d-808a-94cad16bc2